  "title": "Rain, Sun and Cold July: Talking Weather",
  "category": "weather",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "level": 3,
  "order": 302,
  "theme": "weather",
  "culturalNote": "Kenya sits on the equator, so there's no winter or summer — there are rains. The long rains (masika) fall from March to May, the short rains (vuli) from October to December, and the dry season (kiangazi) comes in between. Nairobi is high up, so July is surprisingly cold and grey; Kenyans in jackets and scarves will tell you \"ni baridi sana!\". Weather is the safest small talk there is.",
  "startingPoint": "A grey July morning. You meet your neighbour Mama Njeri at the gate, wrapped in a shawl.",
